A||||2||capeyus||Replacing Clutch||||1||
Z||000000||capeyus||05-26-2001||12:02 AM||||I'm replacing the clutch on an '88 notchback, 4cyl. I just wanted to get some input about the procedure. The book I have is kind of confusing. It says to change the clutch plate you can pull the tranaxle without pulling the motor, then says if you want to change the throwout bearing(duh) you have to drop the engine. I was wondering if anyone could set me straight. I've never worked on a Fiero before but we picked it up cheap and it looks like it might be a boot in the rear to drive. I've done a lot of various procedures on cars (including clutches) I find that experience is the best teacher and I hope to get a few words of wisdom from you folks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||05-27-2001||09:17 PM||||Ok, here is how I would go about replacing a clutch on a 4cyl fiero.<P>major points only.<P>support engine from LHS strut tower to battery. Yes I put the engine weight on rhs on the battery.  <P>I separate the lower ball joints and pull the axles out of the transmission by pulling strut and knuckle assembly. I leave axles still in hubs and struts and hubs and brakes all attached to vehicle other than parking brake cables.<P>I try to only loosen frt cradle bolts. I remove rear cradle bolts so I can swing cradle down on frt bolts.<P>The exhaust I remove from just before converter and get it completely out of the way. In one piece.<P>Now after all the other little stuff I lower the LHS or rear of engine to increase clearance of trans from lhs frame.  <P>The release bearing is attatched to the transmission and comes out with the transmission.  <P>Hope this helps.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||05-28-2001||09:25 AM||||Now I'd drop the whole thing just for fun but here is a web site with some tips for you. <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#trany</A> ||||reg||1
